#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116506 :tutorial/content "Consider the function $y=f(x)$ whose graph is shown below. How can we plot the graph of $y=f(2x)$?\n\n![](images/1a/1a11bef66d0529418dde00e5713ed5e98f53a948c8a60f83fd056fd9e2c18698.png)\n\nWe can use some points on the graph of $y=f(x)$ to find the corresponding points on the graphs of $y=f(2x)$.\n\nFor example, let's take the point $(-2,0)$. The fact that it lies on the graph of $y=f(x)$ means\n\n$$\nf(-2)=0\n$$\n\nThen, if\n\n$$\nx={\\color{red}-1}\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\nf(2x) = f(2\\cdot({\\color{red}-1})) = f(-2) = 0\n$$\n\nThis means the point $({\\color{red}-2},0)$ that lies on $y=f(x)$ has a corresponding point $({\\color{red}-1},0)$ that lies on $y=f(2x)$.\n\nSo, for each point on the graph of $y=f(x)$, there will be a point on the graph of $y=f(2x)$ with the *same* $y$-value and *one half* the $x$-value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}Points on [math]y=f(x)[/math] & Points on [math]y=f({\\color{red}2}x)[/math] \\\\ (-2, 0) & (-1, 0) \\\\ (0, 2) & (0, 2) \\\\ (1, 2) & (0.5, 2) \\\\ (4,-3) & (2,-3)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can plot the points and draw the graph of\n\n$$\ny = f(2x){:}\n$$\n\n![](images/5d/5df5e404864825a7236bc553a401cb83d43ad00d67706845e90872aca4fa5d12.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(2x)$ is the graph of $y=f(x)$ **horizontally scaled** by a factor of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, we can say that the original graph is **compressed** by a scale factor of $2$.\n\nIn general, the transformation $y=f(kx)$ scales the graph of $y=f(x)$ horizontally by a factor of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{k}\n$$\n\n- If $k$ is greater than $1$, the graph is compressed (or squished) towards the $y$-axis by a scale factor of $k$.\n- If $k$ is between $0$ and $1$, the graph is stretched away from the $y$-axis by a scale factor of $k$." :tutorial/id #uuid "0f9522aa-685f-5492-a439-69c534d5bbef" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3285 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}]}
